@jlongster I've put the fix for #46498 here. The local HTTP/SSE and CLI regressions pass, including the direct `api`/`import` requests and terminal connection URLs.

The issue check looks like a false positive: the description already has `Closes #46498`, but the workflow running from `dev` only checks `closingIssuesReferences`, which is empty for this `v2` PR. The body-reference fallback is present in the `v2` workflow but not in `dev`.

Could you link the issue and clear `needs:issue` when reviewing? The fork test workflows also need approval.

OK I not remember what was the exact issue but I think it may be just down the model you use. I think this may come down to just using a model that support  tool calls correctly. Qwen3.6 does.

I use ramalama now instead of ollama and it works fine with opencode

```
		"ramalama": {
			"npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
			"name": "Ramalama (local)",
			"options": {
				"baseURL": "http://localhost:8088/v1"
			},
			"models": {
				"unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q3_K_S": {
					"name": "unsloth Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q3_K_S",
					"limit": {
						"context": 131072,
						"output": 32768
					}
				},
			}
		}
```

however, even though on paper this model I can run on my 9070 XT seems pretty great, I have not tried but using the actual full context would slow it down too much. Free models like Big Pickle are already better than the best coding model I can run. Spend (wasted) a lot of time optimizing all kinds of parameters before I realized how good free models are in comparison.

If course you always pay with your data if you use a remote model. Its kind of sad that we are at a point that free models get you better results then what you can run locally on a pretty expensive consumer GPU but I guess that is where we are.
